---
/**
 * How the royal commissions index is built — draft.
 *
 * A child of /methods/. The parent page states what this site counts and why a
 * register is admitted to it: a stated obligation, a claimant with standing to
 * demand an answer, and a date. A royal commission has none of the three, and
 * this page does not pretend otherwise. What is published here is not a
 * register of things awaiting an answer; it is an index of recommendations and
 * of what a government said about each one, which is the same shape as the
 * committee recommendations index and stands on the same rules.
 *
 * So this page says only what differs. Everything shared — what a stated
 * position is, how a verdict is sorted, what a floor is, editions, corrections
 * — is on the parent and is not restated here, because two statements of one
 * rule are two chances to disagree.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { ROYAL_COMMISSIONS_DRAFT } from "../../lib/draft";
import { fmt, formatDate, formatDateShort } from "../../lib/data";
import {
  commissions, rcFigures, rcLabels, rcSources, rcGrammars, rcRecommendations,
} from "../../lib/royalCommissions";

const all = commissions();
const f = rcFigures();
const labels = rcLabels();
const src = rcSources();
const grammars = rcGrammars();
const pairs = all.flatMap((c) => c.reports.map((r) => ({
  ...r, commission: c.name, only: c.reports.length === 1,
})));
const named = (c: typeof pairs[number]) =>
  c.commission + (!c.only && c.label ? ` ${c.label.toLowerCase()}` : "");
const agree = pairs.filter((c) => c.stated && String(c.found) === c.stated);
const disagree = pairs.filter((c) => c.stated && String(c.found) !== c.stated);
const prose = grammars.filter((g) => g.grammar === "prose").length;
const blocks = grammars.filter((g) => g.grammar === "blocks").length;
const quoted = rcRecommendations().filter((r) => r.governmentWords);
const wordsShown = quoted.length;
const wordsMore = quoted.filter((r) => r.governmentWordsMore).length;
---

<Base
  noindex={ROYAL_COMMISSIONS_DRAFT}
  title="Methods: royal commissions"
  description="Draft: how the royal commissions index is built — where the documents come from, whose words a row carries, what counts as a position, and what this index does not claim."
>
  <p class="draft wrap">
    <strong>Draft.</strong> The index this page describes is not published. Both are
    here to be read and argued with.
  </p>

  <section class="wrap intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methods · royal commissions</p>
    <h1>How the royal commissions index is built</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      {fmt(f.recommendations)} recommendations from {fmt(f.commissions)} Commonwealth royal
      commissions, each in the commission's own words, each with what the Australian
      Government said about it in its own words. This page says how, and what it does not
      claim. It assumes {" "}<a href="/methods/">the methods page</a>, which states the rules
      both indexes share.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="not-a-register">Why this is an index and not a register</h2>
    <p>
      The registers on this site measure one thing: a report was owed an answer by a date,
      and the answer came or it did not. They are admitted because an obligation, a claimant
      and a date can all be established from the record — the Senate requires a response in
      three months, the House in six, and a presiding officer keeps the list.
    </p>
    <p>
      A royal commission has none of those. No statute requires a government to answer one,
      no officer of the Parliament keeps a schedule of the answers, and there is no date to
      be past. So nothing here is overdue, nothing is late, and this index makes no claim of
      that kind at all. What it does is narrower and can be checked: it sets out what each
      commission recommended and what the government said about each recommendation. That is
      the same thing the {" "}<a href="/recommendations/">committee recommendations index</a>
      {" "}does, and it stands on the same test of what counts as a stated position.
    </p>
    <p>
      Elapsed time is shown because it is arithmetic on two tabling dates and not a
      judgement: {pairs.map((c, i) => (
        <>{i ? "; " : ""}{named(c).replace("Royal Commission into ", "")
          .replace("Royal Commission on ", "")} took
        {" "}{fmt(c.daysToRespond ?? c.daysSinceReport)} days</>
      ))}. Nothing is computed against those numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="sources">Where the documents come from</h2>
    <p>
      Only from the copies tabled in Parliament, in the Tabled Documents register on
      aph.gov.au. That is not a preference. Before any of this was built, every document was
      fetched four times into empty directories: the tabled copies came back byte for byte
      identical every time, with no redirects, nothing refused and nothing needing a browser
      or a login. The copies on department websites did not. One host refuses automated
      requests outright, and the other moved department, changed its address, and published a
      file that differs from the one it tabled — a corrected sentence, with no corrigendum to
      say so. A weekly rebuild cannot stand on sources that behave that way, so a stored
      Tabled Documents id is the address of a document here, never a departmental URL.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The test is written up in
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket/blob/main/docs/royal-commissions/source-test-2026-09.md">the source test</a>,
      with every command that produced every number in it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="which-documents">Which documents belong to which commission</h3>
    <p>
      This is kept by hand, in {" "}<code>royal_commissions.csv</code> and
      {" "}<code>rc_seed.csv</code>: {fmt(src.documents)} documents across
      {" "}{fmt(src.commissionsHeld)} commissions, each named with the part it plays — report,
      corrigendum, response, ministerial statement. It is kept by hand because the register
      has no field that says it, and all three rules that look as though they would work do
      not. Twelve of the thirteen records that make up the Disability Royal Commission's
      final report are titled by their volume, so no title rule reaches them. Three of the
      five government responses on file are typed "Other" rather than "Government response",
      so no type filter reaches them. The author field names the commission for fourteen of
      the twenty records typed "Royal commission", is empty for five — including both
      Robodebt records — and names a department for the twentieth.
    </p>
    <p>
      What keeps a hand-kept list honest is that nothing is allowed to arrive silently. Every
      record in the register that is typed "Royal commission", or whose title names one of
      the commissions, and that the list does not hold, is written out as a candidate for a
      person to accept or reject. A rejected record is recorded with the reason —
      {" "}{fmt(src.rejected)} so far, mostly returns to Senate orders about a commission
      rather than documents of one — so the candidate list empties, and the next thing to
      arrive is visible in it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="row">What a row is, and whose words it carries</h2>
    <p>
      One recommendation. Its words are the commission's own, taken from the report, which is
      the opposite of what the committee index does — there the recommendation is read out of
      the government's response, because the committee's report is usually unreachable.
      Here the report is tabled, so the commission speaks for itself and what the government
      said is a separate question on the same row.
    </p>
    <p>
      A row is identified by its report and its number, not by its number alone. A commission
      can recommend twice and be answered twice — the Royal Commission into Defence and Veteran
      Suicide made 13 recommendations in an interim report the government answered in 2022 and
      122 more in its final report, answered in 2024 — and both sets start at one. Which report
      a response answers is kept in the data beside the document, and a response that does not
      say is refused rather than read against everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      How a report numbers its recommendations is counted, not assumed: by chapter
      ("Recommendation 20.5"), straight through ("Recommendation 61"), or straight through with
      no titles at all, which is how one report does it. Whichever form heads more of them is
      the report's, and it is never close. The minority is what the rule keeps out — a bare
      number in the first two reports is a citation of another commission's numbering, and the
      Defence and Veteran Suicide interim report cites the Productivity Commission's three
      times.
    </p>
    <p>
      A recommendation's own heading is told from the recommendation by the type the report
      sets each in, because in print that is the only thing that separates them: Robodebt
      sets a heading in Calibri-Bold above a Calibri body of the same size, and the
      Disability report sets one in DINPro-Medium 13pt above an Arial 11pt body. The
      extraction keeps the font and size of every line beside the text, and a heading is the
      run of lines set the way the label line is set. No font is named in the rule — a
      heading is whatever the label is set in, and the recommendation begins where that
      changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      A recommendation stops at the next recommendation, or at the report's next section
      heading, which is found the same way: a heading in the same face at a larger size. That
      is what ends the last recommendation in a list, where the words alone say nothing —
      Robodebt's 23.8 is followed by "Closing observations", which is a heading in print and
      an ordinary line of text once the type is thrown away. Where a recommendation cannot be
      bounded, the row carries no text and says so rather than guessing; there are
      {" "}{f.textUnreadable === 0 ? "none of those at present" : `${fmt(f.textUnreadable)} of those`}.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      One thing is taken off every quotation on this site, here and on the
      {" "}<a href="/recommendations/">recommendations index</a>: the full stop at the end of
      it. Nothing else is added, removed or reordered, so a row read beside the document it
      came from matches it word for word and differs by that one character.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="counts">The counts, against the reports' own</h2>
    <p>
      A report usually states how many recommendations it makes, and that sentence is checked
      against what was extracted rather than trusted or ignored.
      {agree.map((c) => (
        <>{" "}The {named(c)} says it makes {c.stated}, and {c.stated} is what is there.</>
      ))}
      {disagree.map((c) => (
        <>{" "}The {named(c)} says it makes {c.stated} and prints {fmt(c.found)} under their own
        numbers. {c.unnumbered > 0 && c.found + c.unnumbered === Number(c.stated)
          ? <>The difference is in the report's own list of recommendations. After the last
            numbered one it heads
            {" "}{c.unnumbered === 1 ? "one more item" : `${fmt(c.unnumbered)} more items`} the way
            it heads every recommendation — the same face at the same size, with the
            recommendation set underneath — and gives
            {" "}{c.unnumbered === 1 ? "it" : "them"} no number: “{c.unnumberedWords}”.
            {" "}That is read off the type in the tabled PDF by the same rule that tells a
            heading from the text under it, and only inside the list, where the report bounds
            it: bold at body size is a lead-in, a column head and a chart label everywhere else
            in a report this long. It is still not a row. Every row in this index is keyed by
            the number the report prints, and the check against the government's response looks
            for that number in the response;
            {" "}{c.unnumbered === 1 ? "this item has" : "these items have"} no number to look
            for. Both figures are shown wherever the count appears, and so is what accounts for
            the difference.</>
          : <>Neither figure is adopted here: the shortfall is not a recommendation this index
            is hiding, and nothing in the report has been read that accounts for it.</>}</>
      ))}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The check is what makes it safe to read one document of a report rather than all of
      them. The Disability Royal Commission's final report is thirteen documents and sets all
      of its recommendations out in one of them; a recommendation that lived only in a volume
      would show up as a shortfall against the report's own total, which is what a shortfall
      is for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="positions">What the government said</h2>
    <p>
      The test for a stated position is the one on
      {" "}<a href="/methods/#coverage">the methods page</a> and it is not extended for these
      documents. A government's own verdict word is sorted three ways — accepted, in part or
      in principle, not accepted — and anything else states no position and is shown as the
      government wrote it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The responses read so far answer in two different grammars, and which one a document uses
      is read from the document rather than configured.
      {prose > 0 && (
        <>{" "}{prose === 1 ? "One answers" : `${fmt(prose)} answer`} in prose, the way a
        response to a committee does — "The Government accepts this recommendation" — and the
        register's existing test reads it unchanged.</>
      )}
      {blocks > 0 && (
        <>{" "}{blocks === 1 ? "The other answers" : `${fmt(blocks)} answer`} with a label on a
        line of its own, under a line naming which governments are answering, and the existing
        test finds nothing in it at all, because there is no sentence to find. A response
        carrying those lines is read as blocks: one block can answer a range of
        recommendations at once, and a recommendation can be answered a lettered part at a
        time.</>
      )}
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the parts of one recommendation are answered differently, the row is one
      recommendation and sorts as in part or in principle, with every exact label kept beside
      it. Where the parts do not all state a verdict, the row says so, because the sorted
      words are this site's and the labels are the government's.
    </p>

    <h3 id="named">A recommendation the response names and does not answer</h3>
    <p>
      A response can name a recommendation somewhere other than under an answer to it. The
      Australian Government's response to the Disability Royal Commission answers 172 of the
      222 and lists the other 50 in an appendix, by number and title, under its own sentence
      saying they are "within the sole responsibility of state and territory governments"; its
      reader's guide says the same in the other direction. Read as silence, those
      {" "}{fmt(f.namedNotAnswered)} rows said the response did not address them, of a
      government that had named every one and given its reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are read now, and the rule is the shape of the thing rather than that document: a
      run of at least five lines each opening with a recommendation's number, where none of
      those recommendations is answered anywhere in the response. Below five, a number at the
      start of a line is a cross-reference or a row of a table. The words the row carries are
      the sentence that ends nearest above the list — the list's own caption, in the
      government's words, found by code and checked back against the response like every other
      quotation here. The count is checked too: the response says how many it answers and how
      many it does not, and those two numbers are what this index found.
    </p>

    <h3 id="labels">Every label, and what it was sorted into</h3>
    <table class="register narrow">
      <caption class="sr-only">Each label a government used, and how this index sorts it</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What the government wrote</th>
          <th scope="col">Sorted as</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Recommendations</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {labels.map((l) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{l.label}</td>
            <td data-label="Sorted as">{l.verdict || "no position stated"}</td>
            <td class="num" data-label="Recommendations">{fmt(l.n)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3 id="quotation">How much of the answer is quoted</h3>
    <p>
      The words on a row are the answer as the document prints it, from under the heading
      that names the recommendation to the next heading. What is taken out is the page's
      furniture and nothing else: the running head, which lands in the middle of any answer
      that crosses a page and which is found by asking the Tabled Documents register what the
      document is called and taking the lines that begin the way that title begins. The
      block's own "Responsibility" and "Response" lines are not part of the answer either and
      are shown separately, as the label.
    </p>
    <p>
      An answer longer than about nine hundred characters stops at the end of a sentence and
      the row says the answer goes on — {fmt(wordsMore)} of {fmt(wordsShown)} do. A
      recommendation answered a part at a time carries the first part's words and says the
      same thing, because the other part is its answer too. Nothing is elided inside a
      quotation, and nothing is joined across a heading.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      "Note" and "Subject to further consideration" use no verdict word, so they state no
      position. They are counted as neither an acceptance nor a refusal, and the vocabulary is
      not extended to hold them: a fourth verdict here would stop the two indexes' figures
      meaning the same thing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="governments">Where a recommendation is for more than one government</h2>
    <p>
      {fmt(f.withTheStates)} of these recommendations are answered by the Commonwealth
      together with the states and territories. Where the response gives them separate
      verdicts, which line is the Commonwealth's is decided by the government the response
      names on it and by nothing else: "Response", "Joint response", "Australian Government
      Response" and "Commonwealth" speak for it, and a list of states that does not name it
      does not. The states' verdicts are recorded beside it, because the document says them,
      and they are not counted here: this is a Commonwealth index. Where the Commonwealth is
      not named on any line, no Commonwealth position is shown, even where every state
      accepted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="vocabulary">Where this vocabulary differs from the committee index</h2>
    <p>
      One term here has no equivalent on the committee index, and two more mean something
      different there. Reading across the two without knowing that would mislead.
    </p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div>
        <dt>Named, not answered</dt>
        <dd>
          The response names the recommendation somewhere other than under an answer to it,
          in a list of recommendations it answers none of, and the row carries that list's own
          sentence — {fmt(f.namedNotAnswered)} of them, all in the response to the Disability
          Royal Commission, which answers 172 of the 222 and lists the other 50 in an appendix
          as being "within the sole responsibility of state and territory governments". A
          government that says which recommendations it is not answering, and why, has not
          been silent about them.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Not addressed</dt>
        <dd>
          Here: the response does not mention the recommendation at all —
          {" "}{f.notAddressed === 0 ? "no row is one of these today" : `${fmt(f.notAddressed)} of them`}.
          On the committee index, "not addressed individually" means the response answers the
          report without setting its recommendations out one by one, and the recommendation was
          read from the committee's report instead. Neither is a refusal, and they are not the
          same fact.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Noted</dt>
        <dd>
          Here: the response answers the recommendation and states no position —
          {" "}{fmt(f.noted)} of them, most of them "Subject to further consideration". On the
          committee index the word covers the same idea but the wording that produces it is a
          government's prose rather than a label.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Form letter</dt>
        <dd>
          Not used here. The passage-of-time template is a thing governments do to committee
          reports; neither response in this index contains it, and the segment does not appear
          on the page.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="checking">How the index is checked against the documents</h2>
    <p>
      Every row is looked for in the document it names before it is published. A distinctive
      slice of the recommendation has to be in the report's text, with the row's own heading
      above it and the row's own number above that; the government's words have to be under
      the heading the response itself puts over them, and the government's label has to be
      stated under that heading as the whole of what the document states and not the opening
      of it — "Accept" printed where a document says "Accept in principle" is the one
      misstatement on this side that would matter most. A row that cannot be found that way
      is removed and counted, not flagged, so what is published is verified by construction.
      Nothing is removed at present.
    </p>
    <p>
      A check written from the same two documents as the extraction will pass whatever the
      extraction did, and passing everything on its first run is exactly what that would look
      like. So the check is checked: every row is handed a neighbour's number, heading and
      words, and what survives is counted —
      {" "}<code>python3 verify_rc_index.py --control</code>. Every pair refuses a neighbour's
      number and every pair refuses a neighbour's heading. Nearly every one refuses a
      neighbour's words; the few that do not are rows the response answers in the same words
      on purpose, and this check cannot tell those from a mistake, so it names them rather
      than claiming a clean sweep.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that reaches the cached text itself. If a page of a report never made it into
      the cache, the check compares the index against the same gap and passes. So twenty rows
      were read off the tabled PDFs by eye, page image beside published row, and written up —
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket/blob/main/docs/royal-commissions/hand-check-2026-09.md">the hand check</a>.
      All twenty carry the label, heading and text their report prints and the position their
      response states. It found four faults in the quotations that the automatic check could
      not, because in every one of them the published words really were the document's words
      — with the running head attached, or the next block's heading, or the government's
      sentence cut at its own cross-reference. Those are fixed, and the rules that fixed them
      are the ones described above.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="limits">What this index does not do</h2>
    <p>
      It does not say whether anything was implemented. It records the position a government
      stated on a recommendation, which is a different question from what was then done, and
      the site has no source for the second that meets the standard it holds the first to.
      Where a response commits to a date, the sentence is recorded as written and linked;
      nothing is computed against it and no clock runs from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It covers {fmt(f.commissions)} of the {fmt(src.commissionsHeld)} commissions the
      document table holds. The Royal Commission into Defence and Veteran Suicide and the
      Royal Commission on Antisemitism and Social Cohesion are in the table and have not been
      read. State and territory royal commissions are not in scope, and neither are the state
      and territory responses to a Commonwealth commission.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is rebuilt weekly yet. The registers are; this is not, and until it is, a
      figure on these pages is as current as the day it was built and no more.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Corrections, licensing and how this site was made are on
      {" "}<a href="/methods/#corrections">the methods page</a> and apply here unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .draft {
    margin: 1rem auto 0; padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--rule); font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
